{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Among American X/Twitter users, is hostile political expression associated with political engagement and its antecedents, rather than being solely a matter of deprivation, grievance, or personality?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We extend this revisionist\nperspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression\non X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Are the associations between engagement and hostility stronger among users whose feelings toward political leaders are more polarized?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Expectation 2 concerns whether the associations between engagement and hostility are stronger among users with higher\nleader-directed affective polarization.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do users who express more political hostility post more often and have more connections, giving them greater potential reach on X/Twitter?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is associated with greater activity and potential reach on X/Twitter, measured by\nposting frequency and numbers of friends and followers.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We treat this final expectation as exploratory in nature given the lack of existing research.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Online political hostility is associated with the capacities, motivations, and activities usually valued as part of democratic citizenship. Political engagement therefore belongs alongside marginalization and personality in accounts of who expresses hostility online.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These include distal antecedents such as education and internal efficacy, motivational and cognitive indicators such\nas political interest and political knowledge, and more direct indicators such as online and offline participation.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "These\nfindings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based\nexplanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered\ncentral to democratic citizenship.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study linked survey responses from consenting adult members of YouGov’s U.S. web panel to their publicly available X/Twitter posts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All participants were recruited through YouGov’s standing web panel in the United States.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Participants were specifically asked if they wished to share their X handle\nto link their publicly available posts to their survey responses.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "All participants were adults (18 years or older); no minors were included.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The six-wave panel began in January 2019, added participants in September 2020, and ended in January 2021. The authors linked 2,567 respondents to their accounts; analyses using only platform data could include up to 3,643.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We recruited the original set of panelists in January 2019.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "These\nparticipants, along with an additional set of panelists who were included to increase the study’s power in September 2020,\nwere then asked to participate in five additional survey waves, administered at various time points until the final survey in\nJanuary 2021.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period\nwas 2567. For the analyses where we only use X data the maximum number of respondents is 3643.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors collected 9,250,180 posts from March 4, 2019 through January 2021 and used individual panelists, not posts, as the unit of analysis.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The scraping of X commenced on March 4, 2019 and stopped in January 2021. During this period, we managed to\nscrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists. All measures based on X data are aggregated at the level of the individual\npanelist since our unit of analysis is individual panelists and not posts.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The United States was selected as a setting where the associations might be especially visible because of high affective polarization and political distrust.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The data collection focused on the United States specifically because the associations of interest may be especially\nvisible in highly polarized political environments. The United States provided an ideal context given its well-documented\nhigh levels of affective polarization and political distrust during the study period.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The outcome is degrading, malicious, or intimidating expression grounded in political differences.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define online political hostility as the expression of degrading, malicious, and\nintimidating online behavior towards individuals or collectives based on political\ndifferences.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary Super-Unsupervised measure learns word embeddings from panelists’ posts, combines the vectors for “political” and “hate,” scores each post by cosine similarity to the combined vector, and averages the scores by user or by user-period.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we create word vectors based on posts from our panelists using the word2vec",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, we combine the word vectors\n“political” and “hate” into a combined vector that captures the co-occurrence of political and hostile language in the corpus.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Third, we use this combined measure to calculate the cosine similarity between individual posts and the “political hate”\nword vector",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "we create a\nmeasure of a respondent’s general level of political hostility by averaging the hostility scores of all their posts; for the panel\nmodels we compute this average per time unit investigated (wave or month, depending on the analysis).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Validation compared the primary measure with inspection of high- and low-scoring posts, AFINN sentiment, and Perspective API toxicity. The embedding score correlated with both benchmarks but was more specifically tied to political language.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "posts with high Perspective API scores\nconcentrate on profanity and insults, whereas high political hostility scores combine political referents with hostility (e.g.,\nelites/parties plus derogation), matching the construct.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Google’s Perspective\nAPI toxicity scores (r = 0.59), and the AFINN sentiment lexicon (r = −0.62, negative because AFINN codes negativity as\nnegative values)",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors also report validation against 2,469 posts labeled by four experts. The dichotomized score achieved 74% accuracy on a held-out set, compared with a 50% baseline.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The SU approach was also validated against 2,469 hand-labeled posts rated by four expert raters",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, it reached 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline when dichotomized on a held-out test set.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Criterion checks found that the highest-scoring posts contained more references to political actors than the most toxic Perspective API posts, and that a percentile mapping identified rare hate speech better than chance on an already labeled dataset.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "“Trump” appears 4,163 times vs 831 among the 20,000 most toxic (Perspective) posts",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "a simple percentile mapping of SU Hate yields balanced accuracy of.47 (chance = .33) and identifies the rare “hate\nspeech” class 3 × better than random",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "External validation checked whether scores transferred to public TwitterGloVe embeddings and whether the same method could recover expert judgments of news-domain quality.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "After aligning with public TwitterGloVe embeddings,\nSU “political hate” scores correlate r = .83 with scores computed in this external space",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "SU distances from (nytimes+washingtonpost) correlate .84 with expert fact-checker ratings at the domain\nlevel and .90 at the user-average level",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Ecological validation checked whether hostile posts tended to cast the opposing party as the actor and whether aggregate scores tracked election timing in four countries.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Dependency parsing of the\ntop 100k hostile posts shows the out-party as the grammatical actor in the large majority of cases",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "And in Denmark, Sweden, Germany, and Italy, national Political Hostility aggregates rise toward election day and peak\nexactly on that day, then decline, as expected if the measure tracks political conflict salience.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Robustness analyses used AFINN sentiment, Perspective API toxicity, the separate political and hate components of the main measure, and an alternative supervised classifier trained on hand-labeled posts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we use a classical dictionary-based sentiment approach, the AFINN dictionary [64], which assigns sentiment\nscores ranging from −5 (very negative) to 5 (very positive) to individual words.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, we use Google’s Perspective API [66] to detect online toxicity.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Fig 2 provides a replication of the analyses above where the composite dependent variable of online political hostility is\nsubdivided into the two components of “political” and “hate” (i.e., general hostility). In addition, as described above, we\nalso use an alternative supervised classifier of online political hostility based on a sample of hand labelled posts.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Additional checks interacted the general toxicity and sentiment measures with a post-level political relevance score and controlled for each user’s share of political posts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we interact the Perspective API toxicity scores and AFINN\nscores with a political relevance score (based on the cosine similarity between individual posts and a “politics” word vector\ncreated using the same word embedding approach as described above).",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, we have also created a model where we control for the share of political posts among all posts posted by\neach user.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey measured the two distal antecedents with six education categories and three internal-efficacy items, two from the Center for Political Studies and one from the European Social Survey.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Education is measured using six different educational categories",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Internal efficacy is measured using the standard Center for Political Studies (CPS) questions [46]",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "We also use a third item from the European Social\nSurvey that asks whether “I often think it is difficult to make up my mind about political issues.”",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Political interest was measured with a five-point American National Election Studies item, and knowledge was measured as the number of correct answers to five factual questions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Political interest is measured using a standard American National Election Studies (ANES) question asking respondents\nabout their level of political interest on a five-point scale ranging from “Extremely interested” to “Not interested at all.”",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Political knowledge is measured using five factual questions from the ANES, scored as the number of correct\nanswers.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Respondents reported how often they took part in 11 offline and 10 online political activities on the same seven-point frequency scale.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we created an 11-item battery\nbased on previous research [70,71], asking participants on scales ranging from “1 = Never” to “7 = Several times a day”\nhow often they participate in eleven offline political activities",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "To measure online participation, we asked participants on the same response scale how often\nthey engaged in ten online political activities [72,73]",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Leader-directed affective polarization was the difference between respondents’ average 0–100 ratings of three in-party and three out-party politicians, using party identification to determine which leaders belonged to each group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Respondents rated three Republican politicians (Ted Cruz, Donald Trump,\nand Mike Pence) and three Democratic politicians (Joe Biden, Elizabeth Warren, and Bernie Sanders) on a 0–100 feeling\nthermometer.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "We used respondents’ party identification to distinguish in-party from out-party leaders and calculated the\ndifference between average in-party and out-party leader ratings.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The main models adjusted for Big Five personality traits, party identification, income, age, and gender. Personality was measured with the 44-item Big Five Inventory.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We rely on the Big Five Inventory (BFI) consisting of 44 items to measure the five major personality traits [76].",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "All models control for the Big\nFive personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional analyses pooled respondent-wave observations in OLS models with cluster-robust standard errors. Questions and participation varied across waves, and missing values were handled with multiple imputation and full-information maximum likelihood.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The six surveys did not include the same questions,\nand, consequently, not all measures described below are available in each wave. Further, as participants were free to opt\nin and out of the different surveys, the final sample size for each wave differs.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "For the cross-sectional analyses, we use ordinary least squares (OLS) regression with cluster robust standard errors to\naccount for the fact that a single X user can participate across multiple waves. This dataset is organized as a Time-Series\nCross-Section (TSCS) dataset using pooled OLS.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "we therefore\nuse multiple imputation and full information maximum likelihood to estimate missing values in these analyses.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The moderation analysis combined formal interaction models that adjusted for age and gender with descriptive comparisons of users above and below the polarization scale midpoint.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All models control for age and gender but not the full set of controls to reduce problems of multicollinearity.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "The figure illustrates the interaction term from\neach model from the interaction between the engagement indicators and leader-directed affective polarization.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Fig 4 displays the associations separately for users above and below the midpoint\nof the leader-directed affective-polarization scale.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Random-intercept cross-lagged panel models used behavioral engagement and hostility measures to distinguish stable differences between users from changes within the same user over time.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the panel analyses, we use Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM). These models separate\nstable between-person differences from within-person change",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "we use RI-CLPMs with behavioral measures of online political engagement and online\nhostility.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors applied Benjamini–Hochberg corrections and reported Bonferroni-adjusted intervals within four families of tests: primary associations, alternative outcomes, polarization interactions, and activity or reach.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define four separate families of tests: the primary association models,\nthe robustness outcome models, the affective-polarization interaction models, and the exploratory activity/reach models.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Within each family we apply a Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate (FDR) correction and, for the more conservative\nreading requested by the reviewers, also report Bonferroni-adjusted 95% confidence intervals.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Exploratory negative-binomial models examined counts of posts, accounts followed, and followers. The supplement also visualized follower and friend ties among participants.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "these are negative-binomial count models, and their estimates are\nreported on the original count scale.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "whether\nthose who are politically hostile (a) post more, (b) have more friends, and (c) have more followers. A “friend” in X’s usage\nis someone I follow, and a “follower” is someone who follows me.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "we include exploratory descriptive\nnetwork visualizations of the study participants’ follower/friend graph in S1 Appendix",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Political hostility was positively associated with several engagement indicators after adjustment for broad personality, demographics, and party identification. Political interest, political knowledge, and online participation showed the strongest associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the\nstrongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "The associations were obtained after controlling for\nbroad personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The positive pattern held across alternative measures and did not disappear when the authors accounted for how much of each user’s posting was political.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The robustness analyses are broadly consistent with the main pattern, with significant and sizeable associations across\nall three measures.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "if anything the results are slightly stronger when controlling for the share of political posts. This suggests that the results are not simply driven by more politically engaged users posting more about politics.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Stronger associations among users with more polarized feelings toward political leaders were clearest for online and offline participation. Eight of eighteen interactions survived false-discovery-rate correction, with the surviving results concentrated among participation indicators.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Formal interaction models (Fig 3) show\nthe clearest support for online and offline participation",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "of the eighteen interaction terms, eight\nremain significant after a Benjamini–Hochberg correction, and these are concentrated among the participatory engagement indicators.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "When users became more politically engaged online, their later hostility rose on all three measures. There was also weaker, less consistent evidence that hostility preceded increases in engagement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In brief, within-person increases in online political engagement\nare followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this\npattern is weaker and less consistent.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "More hostile users posted more and had more followers and, to a lesser extent, followed more accounts. The models associated a move from average hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above average with about 420 to 900 posts and about 220 to 370 followers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers\nof followers and, to a lesser extent, friends.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above\nthe mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the\nnumber of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The U.S. X/Twitter results may not carry over to platforms with different audiences, norms, moderation, or features. The Danish X/Twitter results offer only tentative evidence of broader applicability.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, the data come from American X/Twitter users.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Cross-platform research is needed to assess\nwhether similar patterns appear on Facebook, Reddit, YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, or private messaging environments.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Some tentative evidence of broader applicability is provided in S1 Appendix, where online political hostility in a sample of\nDanish X/Twitter users is also positively associated with users’ political interest, internal efficacy, and news consumption",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not show that engagement causes hostility. Within-person models address stable differences between users but cannot rule out time-varying confounding, measurement error, or reciprocal relationships.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Time-varying confounding, measurement error, and reciprocal relationships may remain.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "our evidence does not establish that engagement causes hostility.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not establish how the engagement indicators causally relate to each other or show that education makes users more hostile independently of engagement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "will make no attempt to adjudicate among alternative causal models regarding the relationships between these different\nindicators and measures.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "An important caveat in this respect is that the analyses should not be taken to imply\nthat, for example, more educated users are more hostile absent their generally higher levels of political engagement.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The moderation analysis does not identify a causal effect of polarization on the engagement–hostility relationship; polarization could precede, accompany, or follow hostile interaction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "leader-directed affective\npolarization may be an antecedent, correlate, or consequence of hostile online interaction. We therefore interpret these\nanalyses as suggestive evidence of conditional associations, not as causally identified moderation effects.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization measure concerns feelings toward political leaders, not toward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters. The study does not show whether the same conditional patterns hold for those other targets.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The moderation analyses should therefore\nbe interpreted as evidence about leader-directed affective polarization. Future research should examine whether the same\nconditional patterns appear using party-directed or supporter-directed measures.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The validated embedding score is still an imperfect proxy: it may partly capture politically salient conflict vocabulary rather than hostility itself.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The measure may partly capture\nproximity to politically salient conflict language or hate-related political vocabulary rather than hostility alone.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study excludes nonpolitical toxicity and does not purport to measure hate speech specifically or distinguish all its types and targets.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Accordingly, toxic online exchanges in the aftermath of, say, a\nsports game fall outside the purview of what we examine here.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we do not wish to confuse this more general measure of online political hostility with the narrower concept of “hate speech” which we are not purporting to measure.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "a much greater conceptual and empirical delineation of types and targets of hate speech is called for [69].",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not decide between grievance-based and psychological explanations. In particular, controlling for the Big Five does not directly test accounts based on Dark Triad traits, sadism, or other antagonistic traits that the survey did not measure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "These controls are not intended as a direct test of Dark Triad,\nsadism, or other antagonistic-trait accounts of online hostility. The survey did not include measures of those more specific\ntraits, and we therefore cannot adjudicate those accounts directly.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Posting and connection counts indicate activity and potential reach, not actual network influence, formal centrality, or community structure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These results suggest greater activity and potential reach, but they\nshould not be interpreted as evidence of formal network influence or centrality.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "we do\nnot draw conclusions about formal network influence, centrality, or community structure.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors build on classical political-engagement theory and combine it with revisionist work on motivated reasoning and polarization. They extend the revisionist account of engagement’s costs to hostile political expression on social media.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we\nfind that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, we extend\nand integrate the study of the negative side of political engagement into the study of online politics with a specific focus on\nonline political hostility.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors retain the distinction between distal resources, motivation and knowledge, and participation instead of treating them as interchangeable signs of a single engagement resource.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This distinction follows the theoretical framework above and avoids treating all\nvariables as interchangeable indicators of a single resource construct.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "we will\nin the present study treat them as separate indicators as they may not operate through the same pathways.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Gabriel Almond and Sidney Verba argue that democratic citizenship requires involvement tempered by restraint. The authors build on that warning by examining whether political engagement can accompany more contentious, rather than more civil, online exchanges.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the democratic citizen is called on to pursue contradictory\ngoals: he must be active, yet passive; involved, yet not too involved; influential, yet deferential.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Accordingly, political engagement and its\nantecedents may be associated with rather than diminish online political hostility.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Almond GA, Verba S. The civic culture: Political attitudes and democracy in five nations. Princeton University Press. 2015.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
